{{Short description|List of traditional and fusion dishes from the Baliscan Confederation}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=January 2026}}

The '''cuisine of [[Balisca]]''' features a highly distinct blend of indigenous [[Kurupiranga]] and Wallche ingredients — heavily influenced by traditional [[West Kaftia|West Kaftian]], [[Imfezzan peoples|Imfezzan]], and [[Mesogean world|Mesogean]] staples — fused with the spice traditions, wok techniques, and legume-based cuisines brought by immigrants from Kaftia, Fosia, [[Mesogean Levant|the Levant]] and the rest of the Mesogean basin. The following table lists notable dishes that characterize the fusion palate of the country.

==Notable dishes==
'''Please note:''' some dishes may be listed twice on seperate tables (i.e. popular desserts and snacks)

===Araucaria===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable dishes of Araucarian cuisine
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Mukuta clay pot.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Mukúta'''}} (gathering pot) || The foundational communal stew of Araucaria, cooked in large clay pots over open fire during community assemblies. Cassava, smoked fish, plantain, and black-eyed peas simmer in a deeply spiced broth of ''pimentón de palo'' (chilies smoked over native hardwood), dried shrimp, and the indigenous herb ''ñuú'' (fragrant, with notes of lemongrass and mint). Always prepared collectively — different households contributing ingredients — and served directly from the pot with ''kumú'' cassava flatbread. The highland variation incorporates goat and potatoes.
|-
| [[File:Kumu flatbread.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kumú'''}} (flatbread) || The Araucarian staple carbohydrate — a flatbread made from cassava flour (''harina de mandioka''), grilled on a clay griddle. Served alongside stews, grilled meats, or used as a wrap for fillings. The act of breaking and sharing kumú is a customary gesture of hospitality and communal solidarity, with origins in both South Kaftian and Kurupiranga traditions. In [[Juyú]] and [[Brasil]], kumú enriched with coconut milk and filled with spiced shredded meat is known as ''kumú reyeno'' (stuffed flatbread).
|-
| [[File:Patacon_reyeno.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Patacón reyeno'''}} (stuffed plantain) || Araucaria's most ubiquitous street food — a fried plantain cup filled with shredded spiced meat, black beans, pickled onion, and a sharp ''ají'' sauce. Originated as a Maroon preparation and has since spread across the entire confederation with regional variations in filling and sauce. Sold from stalls and carts at markets, festivals, and outside ''komidas'' (communal dining halls).
|-
| [[File:Waji rice pindora.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Waji de pindóra'''}} (Pindoraman rice) || A rice dish cooked in the South Kaftian style with tomato, pepper, and smoked fish, served with fried plantain (patacón) and akara. The Araucarian adaptation of the Mesopotàmian ''waji rice'', incorporating Pindoraman smoked fish and the local ''ají de pindorama''.
|-
| [[File:Tajin pindorama.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Tajín de pindorama'''}} (Pindoraman tagine) || An Imfezzan-style tagine of chicken with preserved lemons, local olives, and ''quirina'' (feijoa) for sweetness, served over maize couscous rather than semolina. A dish of the Imfezzan-Baliscan community of Pindorama, reflecting the adaptation of Kaftian techniques to archipelago ingredients.
|-
| [[File:Bolo mandioka dessert.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Bolo de mandioka'''}} (cassava cake) || A dense, moist cassava cake flavored with coconut, typically served with guava or passionfruit syrup. Of South Kaftian origin and a staple of Araucarian celebrations and communal meals. The variation ''bolo de kóko'' (coconut cake) — enriched with coconut cream and topped with toasted coconut — is associated with the coastal communities of [[Brasil]].
|-
| [[File:Kokada_dessert.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kokada'''}} (coconut candy) || A coconut candy combining South Kaftian coconut confection techniques with local palm sugar and passionfruit. Sold in small rounds at markets and festivals, particularly associated with the [[Juyú]] highland communities.
|-
| [[File:Akara kumu.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Akara de kumú'''}} (bean fritter on flatbread) || Black-eyed pea fritters served on ''kumú'' flatbread with a sharp pepper sauce. The standard market snack of Araucaria — crisp, golden-brown akara with a fluffy interior, folded into warm cassava flatbread and drizzled with a thin, fiery green pepper sauce.
|-
| [[File:Mukuta yerva.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Mukúta de yerva'''}} (herb pot) || The plant-based version of the Araucarian communal stew, replacing smoked fish with forest mushrooms — particularly the dark-capped ''xongó'' mushroom native to the Araucarian highland forest. The mushrooms are dry-seared to develop deep umami, and smoked ''ñuú'' provides a savory, herbaceous depth. Prepared and served communally, exactly as the fish-containing mukúta.
|-
| [[File:Kachas_sugarcane_bev.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kachas'''}} (burn) || The Baliscan sugarcane spirit, distilled primarily in Araucaria and Pindorama. Consumed neat or as the base of most Baliscan cocktails. The aged variety, ''kachas de karamelo'' (caramel burn), is matured in native hardwood barrels, acquiring a caramel color and vanilla-toast character.
|}

===Bayara===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable dishes of Bayaran cuisine
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Kuxina de mar.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kuxina de mar'''}} (seafood kitchen) || The signature dish of the Salsipuedes coast — whole fish marinated in a Burawi-style citrus-herb paste of lime, garlic, coriander, and ''ñuú'', grilled over hardwood, and served with ''kumú'' and a salsa of mora (blackberry) and chili. The cold, nutrient-rich waters of the Salsipuedes yield the prized ''pejerey'', a cold-water fish unique to the Aurora Current ecosystem, which is the traditional choice for this preparation.
|-
| [[File:Asado_de_kampo.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Asado de kampo'''}} (country roast) || A communal barbecue of beef ribs, chorizo, and morcilla cooked over native hardwood coals — the centerpiece of Bayaran social gatherings. Accompanied by ''kimixi'' (maize-and-cheese bread), roasted peppers, and a sharp ''chimixurí'' sauce of herbs, chili, and vinegar. The Bayaran asado tradition emphasizes simplicity: the quality of the pasture-raised beef is paramount, and elaborate marinades or sauces are viewed with suspicion.
|-
|[[File:Arroz chaufa.png|250x250px]]
|{{lang|bl|'''Arroz chaufa'''}} (stir fried rice)
|Golden-brown fried rice on a plate, stir fried with with soy sauce and sesame oil, scrambled eggs, along with strips of browned chicken or pork, bright green scallions, and the pale yellow rounds of briefly cooked plantain. The surface is garnished with fresh ''ñuú.'' It is often served with small dish of red-orange ''ají de kara'' sauce and a wedge of arazá fruit.
|-
| [[File:Pulpo de aurora.jpg|250x250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Pulpo de Aurora'''}} (Aurora octopus) || Octopus, typically caught in from the waters of the [[Salsipuedes Sea]], prepared simply — either grilled over hardwood with olive oil and ''ñuú'', or slow-braised in red wine with onions and herbs. The cold-water environment produces exceptionally tender, sweet octopus that is prized across the country.
|-
|[[File:Kamixa.jpg|250x250px]]
|{{lang|bl|'''Kamixa'''}} (stir-fried noodles)
|The signature noodle dish of the Darasian-influenced areas — fresh wheat noodles stir-fried with local vegetables, chicken or beef, ''arazá'' fruit, and a sauce of fermented black bean and ají. Served at communal canteens and street stalls, with a small dish of ají sauce on the side.
|-
| [[File:Pejerey grilled.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Pejerey'''}} (sand lance) || The prized cold-water fish unique to the Aurora Current ecosystem, considered the finest table fish in the confederation. Prepared grilled whole with citrus and herbs, or filleted and served in ''arroz de mar''. Its firm, sweet flesh and clean flavor make it the defining ingredient of both the Salsipuedes and Aethiopic coastal cuisines.
|-
| [[File:Chimixuri_sauce.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Chimixurí'''}} (herb sauce) || The Bayaran condiment — a sharp, herbaceous sauce of finely chopped herbs, chili, vinegar, and garlic, served alongside asado and grilled meats. Distinct from the Mesogean chimichurri in its use of local ''ñuú'' and the addition of toasted cumin.
|-
|[[File:Bolate.jpg|250px]]
|{{lang|bl|'''Bolate'''}} (ball [tapioca] tea)
|It consists of brewed tea, milk or plant-based milk, [[W:Smoothie|smoothie]]/[[W:Slushie|slushie]], sweetener, and chewy [[W:Tapioca pearls|tapioca pearls]] (''perlas de tapioka'' or ''bolitas'') that are sucked up through a wide straw. The drink is the signature beverage of the [[West Coast of Balisca]].
|}

===Castejón===
{| class="wikitable"
|+ Notable dishes of Castejón cuisine
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Arroz_de_mar.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Arroz de mar'''}} (sea rice) || The dish most strongly associated with Castejón and the [[Silicón Coast (Balisca)|Silicón Coast]]. A wide, shallow-pan seafood rice combining Volisanian rice-cooking techniques with Bayani spice profiles — saffron, cumin, and the distinctive dried lime (''lima seka''). Features the catch of the Aurora Current: cold-water prawns, mussels, clams, and pejerey. Cooked and served communally, directly from the pan. A thin crust of caramelized rice — the prized ''sokará'' — forms at the bottom. The Bayani dried limes and indigenous ''ñuú'' give it a distinctly Baliscan character that separates it from its Mesogean paella ancestry.
|-
| [[File:Pinxitos_castejon.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Pinxitos'''}} (little skewers) || Small skewers of grilled meat or seafood — the Castejón pinxito tradition is distinct from the simpler preparations found elsewhere. It incorporates Bayani spice rubs (cumin, coriander, ají), Imfezzan preserved-lemon marinades, and Darasian five-spice glazes, reflecting the city's multicultural character. Pinxito bars in the [[Alpujarra]] waterfront district are a celebrated institution, where workers and residents gather after the workday.
|-
| [[File:Empanada_baliscano.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Empanada baliscano'''}} (Baliscan pastry) || A crescent-shaped pastry with a crimped edge, found in every community with variations in dough (maize, wheat, or cassava), filling, and cooking method. The Mesogean style is filled with beef, olives, and hard-boiled egg; the Bayani style with chicken, cumin, and raisins; the Araucarian style with fish, cassava, and ají. The Concordia empanada is larger and typically baked with a flaky Andalusian-influenced crust.
|-
| [[File:Pastel guayaba dessert.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Pastel de guayaba'''}} (guava pastry) || A flaky Mesogean-style pastry filled with strawberry guava paste and glazed with a Bayani-inspired rosewater syrup. The most widely consumed sweet in the confederation and the standard accompaniment to afternoon ''café baliscano''. Sold in bakeries, ''komidas'', and street stalls across the Levante.
|-
| [[File:Flan_mangostino.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Flan de mangostino'''}} (mangosteen flan) || A crème caramel flavored with mangosteen — the signature dessert of the Silicón Coast, blending Monsoran custard techniques with the tropical fruit of the Aethiopic coast. Pale lavender-pink with flecks of mangosteen flesh, silken and trembling, served chilled.
|-
| [[File:Churro guayaba.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Churro de dulxe de guayaba'''}} (sweet guava churro) || A ridged, golden-brown churro with a crisp exterior and soft interior, filled with a core of ruby-red strawberry guava paste and rolled in cinnamon sugar. A popular sweet snack sold from street carts across the Levante, particularly in the Castejón boroughs.
|-
| [[File:Kerveza_araza_bev.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kerveza de arazá'''}} (arazá beer) || A wheat beer brewed with ''arazá'' fruit, producing a tart, fruity profile unique to the archipelago. Pale gold with a thick white head and a faint orange tint from the fruit. The local brewing cooperative ''[[Téchnics en cerveza]]'' is the most celebrated producer.
|}

===Cerdanya===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable dishes of Cerdanyan cuisine
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Verduras a la brasa.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Verduras a la brasa'''}} (grilled vegetables) || Spring onions, sweet potato, mushrooms, quince, eggplant, peppers — grilled over charcoal and served with ''[[salsa de aguaymanto]]''. "The garden's abundance, prepared with absolute simplicity."
|-
|[[File:Pintxo kabaña.jpg|250x250px]]
|{{lang|bl|'''Pintxo de kabaña'''}} (cabin skewer)
|The culinary emblem of Cerdanyan pastoral identity — slow-roasted goat marinated in a herb paste of rosemary, thyme, and local mountain herbs, served with roasted potatoes and a golden-orange sauce made from ''aguaymanto'' (groundcherry). Traditionally prepared for communal celebrations in the high villages of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], the goat is scored deeply, the herb paste rubbed into every crevice, and roasted slowly for four to five hours until falling from the bone.
|-
| [[File:Keso_oveja.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Keso de oveja'''}} (sheep cheese) || Sheep's milk cheese aged in mountain caves — a staple of the Cerdanyan table, with a firm, crumbly texture and a deep, nutty flavor from the mountain herbs in the sheep's pasture. The companion cheese, '''''keso de flor''''' (flower cheese) — a soft cheese wrapped in wild herb leaves — is milder and creamier, with the herb leaves imparting a fragrant, floral rind.
|-
| [[File:Torta de espinaka.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Torta de espinaka'''}} (spinach cake) || A rustic, free-form tart with a golden, flaky pastry crust that is folded over the filling in a rough, uneven rim — the Mesogean ''crostata'' style. The filling is a layer of wild mountain spinach, studded with pale pine nuts, dark raisins, and crumbles of white ''keso de flor'' (or, in the vegan version, a golden cashew cream that is browned slightly in the oven). The tart is cut into thick wedges and served warm, often alongside a simple salad of bitter mountain greens dressed with olive oil and lime. It is the most iconic dish of the Cathar ''[[komida de kasta]]'' tradition — a complete meal in a single pastry.
|-
| [[File:Kuexadu_mora.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Kuéxadu de mora'''}} (blackberry crumble) || A rustic blackberry crumble combining Volisanian baking techniques with the abundant wild ''mora'' (blackberries) of the Matarrañya slopes. Served warm with a scoop of ''krem de quirina'' — quirina (feijoa) ice cream — melting slowly on top. A staple of Cerdanyan mountain inns.
|-
| [[File:Chicha_mora.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Chicha de mora'''}} (blackberry chicha) || A lightly fermented blackberry drink of indigenous Kurupiranga origin, prepared by fermenting mora berries with maize and honey in clay vessels. Tart, slightly effervescent, and deep purple. The customary drink at Cerdanyan communal celebrations and an essential part of highland cultural identity.
|-
| [[File:Arroz de muntanya.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Arroz de muntanya'''}} (mountain rice) || A Cathar-heritage mountain rice with wild mushrooms — particularly the indigenous ''xongó'' — fresh ñuú, and toasted pine nuts or cashew pieces. Pale green and herb-flecked, with a deeply forestal aroma. Served during fasting periods and as a simple communal meal in the highland communities.
|}

===Estuaria===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable dishes of Estuarian cuisine
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
|[[File:Waji rice.jpg|250x250px]]
|'''''Waji rice'''''
(Waajuri rice)
|The signature dish of Estuaria — a savory rice dish that developed in the city of [[Navassa]] fusing together West Kaftian, Volisanian and Andalusian influences, consisting mainly of meat and/or seafood, and vegetables mixed with rice and spices, and the resulting dish is now considered a cross between [[Ayori rice]] and Volisanian [[W:Paella|paella]]. 
|-
| [[File:Fesolada.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Fesolada'''}} (bean stew) || White bean stew slow-cooked with olive oil, garlic, local herbs. There is a meat version that incorporates salt cod. This version is distinguished by the addition of ''arazá'' fruit, which lends a tart sweetness to the broth. A staple of communal dining halls across the interior, served with a drizzle of raw olive oil and thick crusty bread.
|-
| [[File:Pexada_river.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Pexada'''}} (fish stew) || A fish stew made with river fish, plantain, coconut milk, and ''ají'', combining South Kaftian and indigenous techniques. A staple of riverside communities along the [[Marañón basin]], served with rice.
|-
| [[File:Dorado_rio.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Dorado de río'''}} (river dorado) || The prized river dorado, grilled over coals and served with cassava and a salsa of passionfruit and chili. The freshwater jewel of the [[Marañón basin|Marañón]], considered the finest river fish in the confederation.
|-
| [[File:Roti_kari.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Roti de kari'''}} (curry bread) || A flatbread wrapped around a curry of local vegetables, goat, or river fish — the ubiquitous street food of the Kemali-Baliscan communities of [[Pasaña]] and [[Calabazas]]. The Kemali-Baliscan curry tradition uses local ingredients: ''ñuú'' stands in for curry leaf, ''arazá'' for tamarind, and a local variety of mustard seed for the Kemali original.
|-
| [[File:Fesolada_pura.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Fesolada pura'''}} (pure bean stew) || The pure vegetarian version of the Estuarian staple, without salt cod — in many Estuarian ''komidas'', this is the default preparation, with salt cod offered as an optional addition. The arazá fruit's tart sweetness is more pronounced without the fish.
|-
| [[File:Kari pindora.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kari de pindóra'''}} (Pindoraman curry) || A Kemali-Baliscan coconut vegetable curry with ''ñoó'' leaves (the local curry-leaf equivalent), served with rice and ''achar de arazá'' — a tart, spicy pickle made from arazá fruit. The cornerstone of the Kemali-Baliscan vegetarian tradition.
|}

===Hesperia===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable dishes of Hesperian cuisine
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Sancoxo.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Sancoxo'''}} (soup-stew) || The great Baliscan fusion dish and the most widely consumed in the confederation — a hearty soup-stew of meat (typically beef or goat), tubers, plantain, corn, and cassava, simmered with a Volisanian ''sófrito'' of tomato, onion, garlic, and ''ají'', enriched with South Kaftian palm oil and Bayani spices. Every community, every family, every ''komida'' has its own recipe, and debates over the "correct" preparation are a national pastime. Regional variations include ''sancoxo de mar'' (sea soup-stew), ''sancoxo de kampo'' (country soup-stew), ''sancoxo de montanya'' (mountain soup-stew), and ''sancoxo de pindorama'' (Pindoraman soup-stew).
|-
| [[File:Sancoxo yerva.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Sancoxo de yerva'''}} (herb soup-stew) || The plant-based version of the national stew, with dried ''xongó'' mushroom powder providing the umami depth that replaces meat. Chickpeas or white beans are added for protein. Served in most ''komidas'' as a standard ''komida de terra'' option.
|-
| [[File:Empanada_baliscano.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Empanada baliscano'''}} (Baliscan pastry) (Concordia style) || The Concordia empanada is larger than those found elsewhere and typically baked rather than fried, with a distinctive flaky crust influenced by the Andalusian phyllo tradition. Available in Mesogean (beef, olives, egg), Bayani (chicken, cumin, raisins), and Araucarian (fish, cassava, ají) fillings.
|-
| [[File:Baklava_baliscano.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Baklava baliscano'''}} (Baliscan baklava) || An Andalusian-Bayani phyllo pastry made with local nuts (cashew, macadamia) and a syrup of ''aguaymanto'' (groundcherry) rather than honey, giving it a distinctive tart-sweet character. Most associated with the Andalusian communities of the Levante and a centerpiece of Bayani-Baliscan celebrations.
|-
| [[File:Mazapan_araza.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Mazapán de arazá'''}} (arazá marzipan) || An almond-paste confection flavored with arazá fruit, from the Andalusian tradition adapted with local fruit. A traditional gift during confederal holidays, produced particularly in the [[Almodêvar]] district. Small, round, with a faint yellow-orange tint from the arazá.
|}

===Pindorama===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable dishes of Pindoraman cuisine
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Mokeka claypot.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Mokeka'''}} (clay pot stew) || The signature dish of Pindorama — a seafood stew slow-cooked in a clay pot with coconut milk, tomato, onion, garlic, cilantro, and palm oil, thickened with cassava and served with white rice. Of West Kaftian origin but adapted with Pindoraman seafood — particularly the large freshwater prawns found in the territory's rivers — and the local ''ají de pindorama'', a mild, fruity chili unique.
|-
| [[File:Atole acai.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Atolé de açaí'''}} (açaí warm drink) || A warm, thick beverage made from Pindoraman [[W:Açaí berries|açaí berries]], [[W:Maize|maize]] flour, and spices — the most common variety of the ''atolé'' tradition. Deep purple, too thick to drink quickly, consumed slowly with a spoon or by sipping. The atolé tradition is of indigenous origin and is particularly associated with Pindorama and the Araucarian interior.
|-
| [[File:Kui arroz.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kui de arroz'''}} (rice dumpling) || Steamed rice-flour dumplings filled with sweet or savory fillings — a Pindoraman adaptation of Darasian dim sum using local ingredients. Translucent, slightly chewy wrappers with mushroom-and-vegetable or sweet guava fillings, served in bamboo steamers with a soy-ginger dipping sauce.
|-
| [[File:Mokeka koko.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Mokeka de kóko'''}} (coconut clay pot stew) || The plant-based version of the Pindoraman staple — a coconut-vegetable stew with sweet potato, eggplant, okra, and plantain in a creamy coconut milk and palm oil sauce. The standard ''komida de tera'' offering in Pindoraman communal dining halls.
|-
| [[File:Bolo mandioka dessert.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Bolinho de mandioka'''}} (little cassava cake) || Small, oval cassava fritters — deeply golden, crisp exterior and a dense, chewy, pale yellow interior flavored with garlic and herbs. Naturally gluten-free and vegan. Served at market stalls with a dipping sauce of arazá-chili or garlic-herb oil.
|}

===Popular snacks===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable snacks of the Baliscan Confederation
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Empanada variety.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Empanada baliscano'''}} (Baliscan pastry) || The most ubiquitous snack across the confederation — a crescent-shaped pastry with a crimped edge, found in every community with variations in dough (maize, wheat, or cassava), filling, and cooking method (baked or fried). The Mesogean style is filled with beef, olives, and hard-boiled egg; the Bayani style with chicken, cumin, and raisins; the Araucarian style with fish, cassava, and ají; the vegetarian style with spinach, red pepper, corn, and local cheese or cashew cream.
|-
| [[File:Patacon_reyeno.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Patacón reyeno'''}} (stuffed plantain) || A fried plantain cup filled with shredded spiced meat, black beans, pickled onion, and a sharp ''ají'' sauce. Originated in Araucaria as a Maroon preparation and has since spread across the entire confederation. Eaten in two or three bites, held in the hand, sold wrapped in banana leaf or waxed paper.
|-
| [[File:Pastel_koxinha.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Pastel de koxinha'''}} (little thigh pastry) || A small, teardrop-shaped fried pastry with a smooth, golden-brown exterior and a creamy filling of shredded chicken bound in a soft cheese sauce. The crisp shell gives way to the soft, rich interior — the defining textural experience. Of South Kaftian origin but widely adopted across the confederation.
|-
| [[File:Pinxitos_variety.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Pinxitos'''}} (little skewers) || Small grilled skewers of marinated meat — the Castejón tradition incorporates Bayani spice rubs, Imfezzan preserved-lemon marinades, and Darasian five-spice glazes. A plate of pinxitos is typically a jumble of different varieties, served with a bowl of ají sauce and a cold kerveza or kachas.
|-
| [[File:Akara kumu.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Akara de kumú'''}} (bean fritter on flatbread) || Black-eyed pea fritters served on ''kumú'' flatbread with a sharp pepper sauce — the standard market snack of Araucaria. The akara are crisp, golden-brown, and fluffy inside; the pepper sauce is thin, green, and fiery.
|-
| [[File:Kumu guakamole.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kumú de guakamole'''}} (flatbread with guakamole) || Warm cassava flatbread spread thickly with a vivid green guakamole of mashed local avocado, tomato, red onion, cilantro, lime, and ají. The most common quick meal or snack along the Aethiopic coast, eaten out of hand.
|-
| [[File:Churro guayaba.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Churro de dulxe de guayaba'''}} (churro of guava sweet) || A churro filled with strawberry guava paste and rolled in cinnamon sugar — a popular sweet snack sold from street carts across the Levante.
|}

===Desserts and sweets===
{| class="wikitable sortable"
|+ Notable desserts and sweets of Balisca
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Pastel guayaba dessert.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Pastel de guayaba'''}} (guava pastry) || A flaky Mesogean-style pastry filled with strawberry guava paste and glazed with a Bayani-inspired rosewater syrup. The most widely consumed sweet and the standard accompaniment to afternoon ''café baliscano''.
|-
| [[File:Bolo mandioka dessert.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Bolo de mandioka'''}} (cassava cake) || A dense, moist cassava cake from [[Araucaria]], flavored with coconut and served with guava or passionfruit syrup. Of South Kaftian origin. The variation ''bolo de kóko'' (coconut cake) — enriched with coconut cream and topped with toasted coconut — is associated with [[Brasil]].
|-
| [[File:Flan_mangostino.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Flan de mangostino'''}} (mangosteen flan) || A crème caramel flavored with mangosteen — the signature dessert of the [[Silicon Coast]]. Pale lavender-pink, silken and trembling, with a deep amber caramel pool on top. Served chilled.
|-
| [[File:Baklava_baliscano.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Baklava baliscano'''}} (Baliscan baklava) || An Andalusian-Bayani phyllo pastry with local cashew and macadamia nuts, soaked in an ''aguaymanto'' (groundcherry) syrup instead of honey. The aguaymanto's acidity cuts through the butter and nut richness, giving a distinctive tart-sweet character.
|-
| [[File:Kokada_dessert.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Kokada'''}} (coconut candy) || A coconut candy from Araucaria — small, round, with a rough golden-brown surface of caramelized palm sugar and shredded coconut. Soft, chewy, and intensely coconutty. The passionfruit variation has a bright yellow streak running through the center.
|-
| [[File:Mazapan_araza.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Mazapán de arazá'''}} (arazá marzipan) || An almond-paste confection flavored with arazá fruit, from the Andalusian tradition. Small, round, pale yellow-orange, with a smooth, slightly tacky surface. A traditional gift during confederal holidays.
|-
|[[File:Bolate.jpg|250px]]
|{{lang|bl|'''Bolate'''}} (ball [tapioca] tea)
|It consists of brewed tea, milk or plant-based milk, [[W:Smoothie|smoothie]]/[[W:Slushie|slushie]], sweetener, and chewy [[W:Tapioca pearls|tapioca pearls]] (''perlas de tapioka'' or ''bolitas'') that are sucked up through a wide straw. The drink is the signature beverage of the [[West Coast of Balisca]].
|-
| [[File:Kuexadu_mora.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Kuéxadu de mora'''}} (blackberry crumble) || A rustic blackberry crumble of Cerdanyan origin, served warm with ''krem de quirina'' (quirina ice cream) melting on top. Deep purple-black, bubbling, with a buttery oat crumble topping.
|-
| [[File:Sorvete_fruta.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Sorvete de fruta'''}} (fruit sorbet) || Fruit sorbets in vivid colors — deep purple ''mora'', bright orange ''arazá'', pale green ''quirina'', deep red strawberry guava, pale yellow passionfruit. Dense, smooth, and intensely fruity, with no dairy to dilute the fruit character. Served in small ceramic cups or on ''kumú'' flatbread in the Araucarian tradition.
|-
| [[File:Tarta_araza.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Tarta de arazá'''}} (arazá tart) || A rustic free-form tart with a golden, flaky pastry base cradling chunky, jammy arazá fruit in a Mesogean ''crostata'' style. The edges of the pastry are folded over the fruit in a rough, uneven rim. Served warm with quirina sorvete.
|-
| [[File:Pudin koko.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Pudín de kóko'''}} (coconut pudding) || A dense, smooth coconut custard with a thin layer of dark caramel — served inverted so the caramel runs down the sides. The vegan version uses agar-agar instead of eggs, producing a slightly firmer, more jelly-like texture. Served chilled, often with fresh strawberry guava or passionfruit.
|}

===Beverages===
{| class="wikitable"
|+ Notable beverages of the Baliscan Confederation
|-
! Image !! Name !! Description
|-
| [[File:Cafe baliscano.jpg|250x250px]]|| '''[[Café baliscano|''Café baliscano'']]''' (Baliscan coffee) || The national drink of Balisca, made from ''Coffea bayanica'' cultivated on [[Cabu Verd]]. A strong, dark brew served in small cups, sweetened with ''papelón'' (unrefined cane sugar) and spiced with cinnamon or a fresh ''ñuú'' leaf. The Pindoraman coffee ceremony — communal preparation and sharing — is a social ritual observed across the confederation.
|-
| [[File:Guasasa bev.jpg|250x250px]]|| '''''[[Guasasa]]''''' (guaso drink) || National cocktail of [[Balisca]], made with [[Aguardiente de Caña|''Aguardiente de Caña'']] (a distilled spirit from fermented sugarcane juice), lime, and a local fruit (typically guava or passion fruit), and ''[[Mel de Caña]]'' (unrefined cane syrup). The drink is prepared by blending the fruit and syrup, then adding the spirit and ice. Rooted in the culture of the [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] community, today the Guasasa is known and consumed across the confederation and is considered a quintessential element of [[Cuisine of Balisca|Baliscan cuisine]].
|-
| [[File:Atole acai.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Atolé de açaí'''}} (açaí warm drink) || A warm, thick, deep purple beverage made from Pindoraman açaí berries, maize flour, and spices. Too thick to drink quickly — closer to a warm smoothie. The most common variety of the indigenous ''atolé'' tradition.
|-
| [[File:Chicha_mora.jpg|250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Chicha de mora'''}} (blackberry chicha) || A lightly fermented blackberry drink of indigenous Kurupiranga origin — deep purple, slightly cloudy, and lightly effervescent. Prepared by fermenting mora berries with maize and honey in clay vessels. The customary drink at Cerdanyan communal celebrations.
|-
| [[File:Limonade quirina.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Limonada de quirina'''}} (quirina lemonade) || A refreshing cold drink made from quirina (feijoa) juice — pale green, tart, tropical, with the quirina's unique flavor (a cross between pineapple, strawberry, and mint). Popular along both coasts during the warm season.
|-
| [[File:Mate yerva.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Mate de yerva'''}} (herb mate) || [[W:Yerba mate|Yerba mate]] of indigenous origin, prepared in a shared gourd with a metal straw (''bombiya'') and passed among a group. The Cerdanyan preparation is distinctive for the addition of dried mora berries to the mate blend, giving the brew a faint purple tint and a subtle berry undertone.
|-
| [[File:Kachas_sugarcane_bev.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Kachas'''}} (burn) || The Baliscan sugarcane spirit — clear and sharp when unaged, or deep amber with vanilla-toast character when aged as ''kachas de karamelo'' (caramel burn) in native hardwood barrels. The base spirit of most Baliscan cocktails.
|-
| [[File:Vi mora bev.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Vi de mora'''}} (blackberry wine) || A crisp, semi-sweet blackberry wine of the Cathar winemaking tradition — deep purple, served chilled as an aperitif or alongside Cerdanyan cheese and mountain herb dishes. The companion ''vi de kabaña'' (cabin wine) is a robust red from Volisanian grapes adapted to the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]].
|-
| [[File:Kerveza_araza_bev.jpg|250px]] || {{lang|bl|'''Kerveza de arazá'''}} (arazá beer) || A wheat beer brewed with arazá fruit — pale gold, hazy, with a thick white head and a tart, tropical aroma. The arazá's acidity cuts through the wheat sweetness with a clean finish. The Castejón cooperative [[Téchnics en cerveza|''Téchnics en cerveza'']] is the most celebrated producer.
|-
| [[File:Koko koko bev.jpg|250x250px]]|| {{lang|bl|'''Koko de kóko'''}} (coconut of coconut) || A warm, thick, golden-brown coconut drink — the Araucarian evening beverage, served at ''komidas'' and gatherings after the evening meal. Sweet, rich, and deeply coconutty, with cinnamon and cloves. The plant-based counterpart to the evening ''café baliscano'' tradition of the Levante.
|}

